WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.720
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:09.600
I am a light transport simulation researcher&nbsp;
by trade, and I am very happy today, because we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:15.440
have an absolutely amazing light transport paper&nbsp;
we’re going to enjoy today. As many of you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:20.400
we write these programs that you can run on your&nbsp;
computer to simulate millions and millions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:26.320
light rays, and calculate how they get absorbed or&nbsp;
scattered off of our objects in a virtual scene.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:33.440
Initially, we start out with a really noisy image,&nbsp;
and as we add more rays, the image gets clearer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:40.320
and clearer over time. We can also simulate&nbsp;
sophisticated material models in these programs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:44.320
A modern way of doing that is&nbsp;
through using these material nodes.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:49.680
With these, we can conjure up a ton of&nbsp;
different material models and change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:55.840
their physical properties to our liking. As you&nbsp;
see, they are very expressive indeed, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:02.800
the more nodes we use, the less clear it becomes&nbsp;
how they interact with each other. And as you see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:08.960
every time we change something, we have to wait&nbsp;
until a new image is rendered. That is very time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:14.240
consuming, and more importantly, we have to have&nbsp;
some material editing expertise to use this.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:20.800
This concept is very powerful, for instance, I&nbsp;
think if you watch the Perceptilabs sponsorship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:26.960
spot at the end of this video, you will be very&nbsp;
surprised to see that they also use node groups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:32.320
but with theirs, you don’t build material&nbsp;
models, you can build machine learning models.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:39.120
What would be really cool if we could just give&nbsp;
the machine a photo and it would figure out how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:46.000
to set these nodes up so it looks exactly like&nbsp;
the material in the photo. So, is that possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:52.800
or is that science fiction? Well, have a look our&nbsp;
paper, called Photorealistic Material Editing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:58.880
With this technique, we can easily create these&nbsp;
beautiful material models in a matter of seconds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:03.440
even if we don’t know a thing&nbsp;
about light transport simulations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:08.320
It does something that is similar to&nbsp;
what many call differentiable rendering.

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:13.440
Here is the workflow, we give it a bunch&nbsp;
of images like these, which were created on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:19.200
this particular test scene, and it guesses what&nbsp;
parameters to use to get these material models.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:25.520
Now, of course, this doesn’t make any&nbsp;
sense whatsoever, because we have produced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:32.080
these images ourselves, so we know exactly what&nbsp;
parameters to use to produce this. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:40.080
this thing seems useless. And now comes the magic&nbsp;
part, because we don’t use these images. No-no!

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:46.320
We load them into Photoshop, and edit them to our&nbsp;
liking, and just pretend that these images were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:52.720
created with the light simulation program. This&nbsp;
means that we can create a lot of quickly, really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:58.720
poorly executed edits. For instance, the stitched&nbsp;
specular highlight in the first example isn’t very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:04.720
well done, and neither is the background of the&nbsp;
gold target image in the middle. However, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:10.560
key observation is that we built a mathematical&nbsp;
framework, which makes this pretending really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:17.600
work! Look, in the next step, our method proceeds&nbsp;
to find a photorealistic material description&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:23.200
that, when rendered, resembles this target&nbsp;
image, and works well even in the presence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:29.360
of these poorly executed edits. So these&nbsp;
materials are completely made up in Photoshop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:34.720
and it turns out, we can create photorealistic&nbsp;
materials through these node graphs that look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:41.440
almost exactly the same. Quite remarkable.&nbsp;
The whole process executes in 20 seconds.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:45.280
If you are one of the more&nbsp;
curious Fellow Scholars out there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:48.960
this paper and its source code are&nbsp;
available in the video description.

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:54.560
Now, this differentiable thing has a lot&nbsp;
of steam. For instance, there are more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:59.760
works on differentiable rendering. In this&nbsp;
other work, we can take a photo of a scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:04.400
and a learning-based method turns the&nbsp;
knobs until it finds a digital object&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:12.080
that matches its geometry and material&nbsp;
properties. This was a stunning piece of work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:18.160
from Wenzel Jakob and his group, of course, who&nbsp;
else. They are some of the best in the business.

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:24.560
And we don’t even need to be in the area of light&nbsp;
transport simulations to enjoy the benefits of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:31.280
differentiable formulations, for instance, this&nbsp;
is differentiable physics. So what is that?

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:36.320
Imagine that we have this billiard game, where&nbsp;
we would like to hit the white ball with just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:42.160
the right amount of force and from the right&nbsp;
direction, such that the blue ball ends up close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:48.560
to the black spot. Well, this example shows that&nbsp;
this is unlikely to happen by chance, and we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:53.760
to engage in a fair amount of trial and error&nbsp;
to make this happen. What this differentiable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:59.280
programming system does for us is that we can&nbsp;
specify an end state, which is the blue ball&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:05.360
on the black dot, and it is able to compute the&nbsp;
required forces and angles to make this happen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:11.600
Very close.
So after you look here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:14.960
maybe you can now guess what’s next&nbsp;
for this differentiable technique…it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:21.680
starts out with a piece of simulated ink&nbsp;
with a checkerboard pattern, and it exerts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:27.440
just the appropriate forces so that it forms&nbsp;
exactly the Yin-Yang symbol shortly after.

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:32.560
And now that we understand what&nbsp;
differentiable techniques are capable of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:39.040
we are ready to proceed to today’s paper. This is&nbsp;
a proper, fully differentiable material capture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:45.840
technique for real photographs. All this needs&nbsp;
is one flash photograph of a real-world material.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:50.720
We have those around us in abundance, and&nbsp;
similarly to our previous method, it sets up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:57.280
the material nodes for it. That is a good thing,&nbsp;
because I don’t know about you, but I do not want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:04.560
to touch this mess at all. Luckily, we don’t have&nbsp;
to, look! The left is the target photo, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:09.840
right is the initial guess of the algorithm,&nbsp;
that is not bad, but also not very close.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:16.480
And now, hold on to your papers and just look at&nbsp;
how it proceeds to refine this material until it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:23.600
closely matches the target. And with that, we have&nbsp;
a digital representation of these materials. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:28.960
can now easily build a library of these materials&nbsp;
and assign them to the objects in our scene.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:36.240
And then, we run the light simulation&nbsp;
program, and here we go. Beautiful.

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:42.960
At this point, if we feel adventurous, we can&nbsp;
adjust small things in the material graphs to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:48.880
create a digital material that is more in line&nbsp;
with our artistic vision. That is great, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:54.480
it is must easier to adjust an already existing&nbsp;
material model than creating one from scratch.

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:59.120
So what are the key differences&nbsp;
between our work from last year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:06.240
and this new paper? Our work made a rough initial&nbsp;
guess and optimized the parameters afterwards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:11.280
it was also chock full of neural networks,&nbsp;
it also created materials from a sample,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:18.480
but that sample was not a photograph, but&nbsp;
a photoshopped image. That is really cool,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:24.000
however, this new method takes an almost&nbsp;
arbitrary photo, many of these we can take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:30.080
ourselves or even get them from the internet,&nbsp;
therefore this new method is more general.

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:36.080
It also supports 131 different&nbsp;
material node types, which is insanity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:40.080
Huge congratulations to the&nbsp;
authors, if I would be an artist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:44.960
I would want to work with this right&nbsp;
about now. What a time to be alive!

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:50.960
So there you go, this was quite a ride, and I&nbsp;
hope you enjoyed it just half as much as I did.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:56.560
And if you enjoyed it at least as much as I did,&nbsp;
and you feel a little stranded at home and are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.560 --> 00:08:01.520
thinking that this light transport thing is pretty&nbsp;
cool, and you would like to learn more about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:06.800
I held a Master-level course on this topic&nbsp;
at the Technical University of Vienna.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:12.160
Since I was always teaching it to a handful of&nbsp;
motivated students, I thought that the teachings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:17.280
shouldn’t only be available for the privileged&nbsp;
few who can afford a college education, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:23.040
the teachings should be available for everyone.&nbsp;
Free education for everyone, that’s what I want.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:28.720
So, the course is available free of charge&nbsp;
for everyone, no strings attached, so make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:33.840
sure to click the link in the video description&nbsp;
to get started. We write a full light simulation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:38.880
program from scratch there, and learn about&nbsp;
physics, the world around us, and more.

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:32.880
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

